WEBVTT

00:00.040 --> 00:00.760
All right, my friends.

00:00.760 --> 00:06.360
So now we're going to talk about how to transform the numeric values inside our data by using two very

00:06.360 --> 00:09.560
simple functions the round and the EPs the absolute.

00:09.560 --> 00:11.360
So let's go and explore them.

00:15.160 --> 00:15.400
Okay.

00:15.400 --> 00:18.240
So now let's have this example three points 516.

00:18.280 --> 00:23.000
Now let's say that you want to apply the function round and you are using two decimal places.

00:23.000 --> 00:27.720
So what can happen is you can go and keep only two digits after the decimal point.

00:27.720 --> 00:31.640
So five and one and the third digit after the decimal six.

00:31.680 --> 00:35.400
It will decide whether the number gonna round up or stay as it is.

00:35.440 --> 00:38.000
And now since six is higher than five.

00:38.040 --> 00:41.680
So that means SQL is going to go around the numbers up.

00:41.680 --> 00:44.880
So instead of having 51 we will get 52.

00:44.920 --> 00:47.480
And after that the third digit is gonna reset to zero.

00:47.480 --> 00:50.280
So in the output you will get 3.52.

00:50.440 --> 00:54.400
Now let's say that you have done round but only for one decimal place.

00:54.400 --> 00:59.600
Now SQL can go and keep only one decimal place and that is the five and the second digit.

00:59.600 --> 01:02.400
This time going to decide whether we round up or not.

01:02.440 --> 01:04.970
And now since one is less than five.

01:05.010 --> 01:08.010
There is no need to round up and the five can stay as it is.

01:08.010 --> 01:13.450
It will not turn to six, so there is no round up and the digits after the five can reset to zero.

01:13.450 --> 01:15.330
So we're going to get 3.5.

01:15.370 --> 01:17.610
Now let's say that you say round zero.

01:17.610 --> 01:21.090
So that means I don't want to see any digits after the decimal point.

01:21.090 --> 01:25.090
So now SQL is going to go and check the first digit after the decimal point.

01:25.130 --> 01:25.730
The five.

01:25.770 --> 01:29.010
This one going to decide whether the three is going to turn to four or not.

01:29.050 --> 01:35.090
And now since we have five, it is good enough to round the number because either five or above five

01:35.130 --> 01:36.770
can round the numbers.

01:36.770 --> 01:38.930
So that's why it's going to be a round up.

01:38.930 --> 01:44.690
And SQL can return at the end four and all the digits after the decimal point can be reset to zero.

01:44.730 --> 01:48.210
So this is exactly how the round function works in SQL.

01:48.210 --> 01:50.690
So now let's see how we can do that in SQL okay.

01:50.730 --> 01:53.130
So now let's go and practice about the number functions.

01:53.130 --> 01:55.410
So what we're going to do we're going to write SQL select.

01:55.410 --> 01:58.250
But this time we will not select any data from the database.

01:58.250 --> 02:04.490
We're going to practice using our static value like for example the value three dots 516.

02:04.530 --> 02:06.010
So let's go and execute it.

02:06.010 --> 02:07.970
So with that I have this decimal number.

02:08.010 --> 02:11.250
Now let's go and start practicing the round function.

02:11.250 --> 02:15.330
So now let's go around this number three dots 516.

02:15.370 --> 02:17.970
And this time we are rounding to decimals.

02:17.970 --> 02:20.570
So let's go and call it round two.

02:20.610 --> 02:22.050
And let's go and execute it.

02:22.050 --> 02:25.850
So as you can see in the output we are rounding to two decimal places.

02:25.850 --> 02:30.010
And we have the two because as we learned the six cannot go and round it up.

02:30.010 --> 02:33.690
Now let's go and do the same thing for one.

02:33.690 --> 02:35.930
So let's round one execute.

02:35.970 --> 02:39.290
And as you can see in the output we are rounding to one decimal.

02:39.290 --> 02:41.370
So we have the five and everything is zero.

02:41.370 --> 02:44.970
And we don't have six here because the one is lower than five.

02:45.010 --> 02:47.170
And it will not round up the numbers.

02:47.170 --> 02:49.490
And let's round by the zero.

02:50.930 --> 02:56.490
It is rounding it to an integer to the four, and all the decimal digits are zero, and we have four

02:56.490 --> 02:59.890
because we have five and five can round up that number.

02:59.890 --> 03:01.370
So as you can see it is really nice.

03:01.410 --> 03:03.570
And this is how we round numbers in SQL.

03:07.610 --> 03:13.060
Now there is another number function which is really cool, called apps or the absolute what it's going

03:13.060 --> 03:16.660
to do, it's going to go and convert any negative number to a positive.

03:16.700 --> 03:18.020
So let me show you what I mean.

03:18.020 --> 03:20.380
Let's go and say we have like minus ten.

03:20.380 --> 03:21.820
So this is a negative number.

03:21.820 --> 03:28.340
But if I say apps so the absolute of the minus ten what I will get I will get a positive number.

03:28.340 --> 03:30.860
So it's like giving us the absolute of any number.

03:30.860 --> 03:34.820
Or in other words it is like converting the negative to a positive.

03:34.820 --> 03:38.420
And if the number is already positive, nothing going to happen.

03:38.420 --> 03:41.940
So if I say the absolute of the ten, I will get as well a ten.

03:41.940 --> 03:47.140
So this is really nice and cool function that is really important in order to transform numbers in many

03:47.140 --> 03:52.940
scenarios, like if you have mistakes on your database, like let's say a minus sales makes no sense

03:52.940 --> 03:54.620
to have sales, that is minus.

03:54.620 --> 03:59.580
So in order to correct the data, we can use the apps in order to convert all the negative numbers to

03:59.620 --> 04:00.300
a positive.

04:00.340 --> 04:03.700
So this is really nice, cool and easy function to learn.

